{
  "dom_id": "step-q216055",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Let $n\\in N.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then $n$ is prime. \n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\nI. $6n$ is prime\n\nII. $2n+1$ is even\n\nIII. $12$ is composite",
  "worked_solution": "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in N,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $n\\in N,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement III is always true. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
